Techniques to Plot Multiple Graphs in the Same Plot:

1. Using hold on / hold off:
After plotting the first graph, use 'hold on' to prevent the plot from being cleared. Subsequent plot commands add to the same axes.

Example:
plot(x, y1);
hold on;
plot(x, y2);
plot(x, y3);
hold off;

2. Plotting Multiple Curves in One plot() Call:
Pass multiple x-y pairs to a single plot() command.

Example:
plot(x, y1, x, y2, x, y3);

3. Using Line Specifiers:
Differentiate curves with color/style within the same plot.
plot(x, y1, 'r-', x, y2, 'b--', x, y3, 'g:');

4. Adding Legend:
Use legend() to label each curve.
legend('Curve 1', 'Curve 2', 'Curve 3');

Putting Multiple Plots on the Same Page using subplot():
subplot(m, n, p) divides the figure window into an $m\times n$ grid and activates the $p$-th panel.

Syntax:
subplot(rows, cols, panel_number)

Example — 4 plots in 2x2 grid:
subplot(2, 2, 1); plot(x, sin(x)); title('sin(x)');
subplot(2, 2, 2); plot(x, cos(x)); title('cos(x)');
subplot(2, 2, 3); plot(x, tan(x)); title('tan(x)');
subplot(2, 2, 4); plot(x, x.^2); title('x^2');

Other Useful Commands for Multi-plot Figures:
- figure() — opens a new figure window
- title(), xlabel(), ylabel() — label each subplot individually
- grid on — adds grid to current subplot
- axis([xmin xmax ymin ymax]) — sets axis limits

Summary:
| Technique | Use |
|---|---|
| hold on/off | Overlay plots on same axes |
| plot(x,y1,x,y2) | Multiple curves in one call |
| subplot(m,n,p) | Multiple panels on same page |
| figure() | New separate figure window |

